      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "We now consider the properties of a subset of the normal distribution, specifically a tail of the distribution (Figure 2.2). Such a consideration is important in the extreme form of directional selection that is used by most plant and animal breeders. Under truncation selection, all individuals below a certain phenotype are culled from the population and hence have zero fitness. The critical phenotype, T, is called the truncation point. For a normally distributed phenotype, the mean phenotype of the population above the threshold (i.e., after selection) can be written as",
      "source_page": 46,
      "printed_page": 26,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p46:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        31.0,
        1058.0,
        1000.0,
        1341.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter2_formula030]]",
      "source_page": 46,
      "printed_page": 26,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p46:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        397.0,
        1344.0,
        618.0,
        1430.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In computing a mean, the phenotype frequencies must sum to one, and this is",
      "source_page": 46,
      "printed_page": 26,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p46:b12"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        25.0,
        1456.0,
        978.0,
        1516.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FIGURE:2.3]]",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        202.0,
        732.0,
        1066.0,
        870.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "accomplished by letting the density of phenotype z after selection be",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        159.0,
        914.0,
        981.0,
        967.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter2_formula031]]",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        571.0,
        990.0,
        710.0,
        1069.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Since the denominator, $ \\int_{T}^{\\infty} p(z) dz $, is the sum of frequencies for phenotypes greater than T (i.e., the fraction of individuals allowed to reproduce), it is a measure of the intensity of selection, and we hereafter denote it as $ \\Phi_{T} $. After integration, the solution to Equation 2.13 is found to be",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        162.0,
        1094.0,
        1125.0,
        1248.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter2_formula032]]",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        558.0,
        1265.0,
        734.0,
        1329.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where $p_T$ is the height of the standard normal curve at the truncation point, obtained by setting $z' = (T - \\mu)/\\sigma$ in Equation 2.11,",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        170.0,
        1350.0,
        1129.0,
        1441.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter2_formula033]]",
      "source_page": 47,
      "printed_page": 27,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p47:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        454.0,
        1455.0,
        848.0,
        1536.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FIGURE:2.4]]",
      "source_page": 48,
      "printed_page": 28,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p48:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        95.0,
        736.0,
        959.0,
        873.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Numerical values for $ \\Phi_{T} $, which are functions of the standardized distance $ z' $ of T from $ \\mu $, are obtainable from tables of the standard normal distribution (see also Figure 2.3).",
      "source_page": 48,
      "printed_page": 28,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p48:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        45.0,
        918.0,
        1004.0,
        1030.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Equation 2.14 states that it the upper proportion $ \\Phi_T $ of a normally distributed population is selected, the mean will advance $ \\mu_s - \\mu = \\sigma_{p_T} / \\Phi_T $ units. This change in the mean caused by selection is often denoted by $ S $, the directional selection differential (Chapter 3). In units of phenotypic standard deviations, the standardized selection differential is simply $ (\\mu_s - \\mu) / \\sigma = p_T / \\Phi_T $, which is plotted in Figure 2.4.",
      "source_page": 48,
      "printed_page": 28,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p48:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        1014.0,
        1000.0,
        1224.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In a similar fashion, the variance of the selected population can be shown to be",
      "source_page": 48,
      "printed_page": 28,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p48:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        1206.0,
        997.0,
        1285.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter2_formula034]]",
      "source_page": 48,
      "printed_page": 28,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p48:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
